closes the gap where every timing table in the repo compared HELUT only to other
HELUT configurations. The clear Swift oracle is a per-bit dictionary interpreter and
is useless as a performance baseline, so 'is tensorising faster than native HDL' was
unmeasured rather than merely unproven.

held constant: one emitted LUT6 artifact feeds both backends (verilator simulates the
  Verilog directly; HELUT executes the $lut netlist yosys derives from it via abc -lut 6),
  identical stimulus in identical order, and both fold outputs into the same FNV-1a
  digest over (lane, wire, value). A digest mismatch invalidates the timings.
excluded: build and compile time on both sides. HELUT is warmed; its cold first-run JIT
  cost is reported separately.
NOT held constant, and the actual question: HELUT evaluates all lanes in one graph
  submission while verilator loops sequentially and single-threaded.

